 essentially look at this idea  and state that if odin had basically  defeated or captured or destroyed  an alien race and then took their  spiritual essence and confined it within  uh what basically became mangog that  even when they were freed that he  unwittingly created a kind of bond  between man gog  and the emotional aspect of hatred anger  fear different things along those lines  and so long as those exist  man god will always exist now of course  over the course of the story going into  196 through 197  man god did exactly what he did before  which was to absolutely  lay waste to the entirety of asgard the  problem with this is that depending on  who you talk to  some people loved the events of in the  shadow of the man gog  other people hated it right it's one of  these stories where it kind of splits  the community but one of the things that  people cannot really deny is that these  early appearances of mangog  had him as an absolute boss right this  guy would just crush  asgard and it's one of these things  where even in these early stories it was  solidified that asgard  couldn't defeat mangog on their own did  they grab different giants that were out  there they even worked with hella at one  point in time  that there were these different  situations where when man gog showed up  it took  basically pulling out all the stops to  bring an end to these guys and it's one  of these things where you got these  small little moments here and there  where  for example man gog was powered with the  hate of a billion billion beings right  just different things along those lines  but having said that the confusion that  existed  in terms of how man god could exist with  this race having basically been returned  and all that kind of stuff  was solved in thor issue number 198 when  jerry conway took  everything and just wrapped it all  together both the work of stanley and  jack kirby as well as the work of  himself  and essentially revealed here that yes  man gog is powered by the hatred  of the beings that were by all standards  of measurement destroyed but the  important thing here is despite the fact  that they had been returned  they still hated odin and so he was  still powered by that hate and so  whether or not those beings had  basically been destroyed and their hate  had essentially  manifested in the form of man gog  himself or they were still alive that  hatred would still exist and so long as  it exists  man gog would exist now the whole point  behind jerry conway doing this again i  couldn't really find a definitive  interview but i kind of have to believe  this was the case  the whole point of doing this was to  basically create a kind of end run so  there'd always be a means by which  man god could return and the reason why  was because in these early appearances  one of the things that was solidified is  that man gog  did have the ability to tap into magical  energy he could shapeshift so on and so  forth but more than anything he was just  an overwhelming force that could never  actually be destroyed hence the reason  that odin made the statement that he  could never actually destroy  mangogog himself because he basically  has to exist at this point he's a force  of nature it would take somebody on a  much higher level of power who in all  honesty would not really have any  interest in destroying the man gog now  having said all of that the question  that you might find yourself asking here  is that if the manga was was introduced  as  a credible threat to asgard so much so  that even odin was afraid of him  and was just kind of this powerhouse  then why did marvel stop writing stories  about it right why didn't he necessarily  appear that much why is it that he  appeared  11 times over the course of those those  you know original thor stories  and then just kind of faded into  oblivion at basically a fever pitch  the reason for that is one of the things  we've talked about before is that  following 1971 a lot of people point to  the dark knight returns at dc comics as  kind of the starting point of the bronze  age where stories became darker and more  cerebral  focusing more on character-driven  stories as opposed to just crazy events  happening  i really point more towards 1971 with  the night when stacy died the first time  in really comic book history that you  saw an instance where a love interest of  a superhero died  not because somebody else killed her in  the same way that like eobard thon  killed  iris west in the 1970s in the flash  comics but by the superhero's  own failure which was spider-man's  mistake then that basically changed  everything in comics and so you started  seeing things going forward where it was  focusing again more on cerebral stories  focusing more on character-driven  stories and less on things like here  comes the man-gog  not only that because the man-god was a  character that had been written a  certain way by stan lee and jack kirby  as well as jerry conway there wasn't  really a whole lot of drive to correct  that there wasn't really a whole lot of  drive  to write him in a more interesting and  intriguing way and so that's why  over the years if you look at things  like thor volume 2 issues 21 through 25  where you look at issue number 84 or you  go back and you look at the thunder  strike series thunderstrike volume 2  issue number five you have these  appearances by man gog that are small  time right where he just kind of pops up  for a little bit and then it's just  almost immediately defeated and it's  always like well you know so long as  hatred will endure i will always endure  and that's basically it right he's just  kind of and a lot of ways treated like a  punching bag and truth to tell  that pisses off man god fans because  while man god doesn't really have  a lot of fans out there the fans he does  have love him and it's one of the things  where they just kind of get pissed at  the idea that marvel just sort of throws  him out there and nothing ever really  happens with him especially because of  the fact that he was a guy that scared  the piss out of odin back in the day  in in marvel comics and so more recently  with man gog and it's something that we  covered under jason aaron's thor run  which i still consider to be the  greatest thor run of all time  but one of the things that had happened  more recently is you saw man gog  returning really at the tail end of the  series  facing off against jane foster and this  wasn't really designed to give us  man gog as we were used to which was a  just massive credible threat that would  call together  really not just thor and the asgardians  but probably a whole host of forces  from across the different realms as well  as probably the avengers on earth  instead it was really more of a story  designed to complement and really focus  on the character of jane foster  that man gog was given as a way to kind  of be a physical manifestation  of how much everybody hated jane foster  and that her  overcoming the man gog was her  overcoming everybody's hatred of her i.e  jason aaron saying sorry about your luck  but she's not going anywhere of course  she did they basically ended the thor  story and then she ended up becoming  valkyrie so  you know one of the things that i always  said during that time is give it a while  odin's son will go back to being thor  again nobody wanted to believe it would  happen  but it always does in comics that's the  nature of comics is always kind of going  in a cyclical pattern but the important  thing here is last we saw man god  basically he was thrown into the sun  and whether or not anything significant  really comes out of that  we don't know it's one of these things  where man gog was defeated and then at  some point we'll likely end up coming  back  and that's going to be the end of it but  that's one of the things is that man gog  was always just this incredible host of  a character who ended up being turned  into more or less a plot device  and a punching bag for whatever  particular story was being written by  that particular writer at the time so  again you know that kind of happens in  marvel comics and it happens a lot more  often than you would like to believe  that you have these characters that are  introduced as just like these insanely  powerful beings  and then are just kind of throwaway  characters after that and just kind of  get beat up on  and that's basically it and they seem  like a shadow of their former selves  in terms of the power they possess but  with that being said guys we're gonna  bring this video to an  end thank you guys for watching and i  will catch you all later  peace  you
 

